{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 310,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Over the course of your life your feet will\nage approximately 1 second more than your"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4830,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "head due to gravitational time dilation - and\nthat’s assuming that your life is long and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10000,
    "dDurationMs": 1060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that you’re quite tall."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11060,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that tiny difference in flow of time may\nbe what keeps you stuck to this planet at"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16100,
    "dDurationMs": 2840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21559,
    "dDurationMs": 3610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Albert Einstein really enjoyed imagining people falling off buildings."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25169,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He said it himself - he described his happiest thought as the following: “For an observer"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29679,
    "dDurationMs": 5241,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "falling freely from the roof of a house, the\ngravitational field does not exist.”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34920,
    "dDurationMs": 4011,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We now know this as the equivalence principle - it states that there’s no experiment that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38931,
    "dDurationMs": 4449,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you can do to distinguish a frame of reference in freefall within a gravitational field from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43380,
    "dDurationMs": 3950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a frame of reference floating off in space\nin the absence of gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47330,
    "dDurationMs": 4650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Provided of course you’re in a lab with\nno windows, and there’s no air resistance,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51980,
    "dDurationMs": 1320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and you haven’t hit the ground yet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53300,
    "dDurationMs": 6070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But otherwise, as far as the universe is concerned, the sense of floating you feel in both circumstances"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59370,
    "dDurationMs": 1260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is exactly the same."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60630,
    "dDurationMs": 4569,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Likewise, the sense of weight you feel stationary on the surface of the Earth is identical to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65199,
    "dDurationMs": 5191,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the sense of weight you would feel accelerating at 1-g distant from any gravitational field"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70390,
    "dDurationMs": 3260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- at least as far as the laws of physics are\nconcerned."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73650,
    "dDurationMs": 5539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einstein had his happy thought in 1907, a\ncouple of years after he started his scientific"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79189,
    "dDurationMs": 3021,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "revolution with the special theory of relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82210,
    "dDurationMs": 4659,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It took him another 8 years and a lot of help\nto grow this simple idea into his full theory"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86869,
    "dDurationMs": 3511,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of gravity - the general theory of relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90380,
    "dDurationMs": 4979,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "General relativity, or “GR” explains the\nforce of gravity as being due to curvature"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95359,
    "dDurationMs": 1791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in space and time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97150,
    "dDurationMs": 4489,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mass and energy change the lengths of rulers and the speeds of clocks - and somehow those"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101639,
    "dDurationMs": 3730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "changes lead to objects being attracted to\neach other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105369,
    "dDurationMs": 5381,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "John Archibald Wheeler put this notion the\nmost pithily: Spacetime tells matter how to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110750,
    "dDurationMs": 3310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "move; matter tells spacetime how to curve."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114060,
    "dDurationMs": 4939,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A common way to depict this is with the classic balls-on-rubber-sheet analogy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118999,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Balls are constrained to move only on the\nsheet, and will move in straight lines if"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122899,
    "dDurationMs": 6750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the sheet is flat - but if the sheet is curved\nthen there are no straight lines."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129649,
    "dDurationMs": 2841,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the rubber sheet picture is at best a\ncrude analogy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132490,
    "dDurationMs": 5789,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For one thing, it implies that curvature in\nthe fabric of space is the cause of gravitation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138279,
    "dDurationMs": 4501,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- but that’s only half - actually less than half the picture."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142780,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Matter tells space AND time how to curve,\nand it’s the curvature of time that’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148150,
    "dDurationMs": 2490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mostly responsible for telling matter how\nto move."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150640,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s a deep connection between gravity\nand time - gravitational fields seem to slow"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156010,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the pace of time in what we call gravitational\ntime dilation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160450,
    "dDurationMs": 3539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And today we’ll explore the origin of this\neffect."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163989,
    "dDurationMs": 4551,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And ultimately, we’ll use what we learn\nto understand how curvature in time - this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168540,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gradient of time dilation - can be thought\nof as the true source of the force of gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174000,
    "dDurationMs": 6079,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It would actually be really helpful if you’ve\nalready seen our recent video on paradoxes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180079,
    "dDurationMs": 1871,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in special relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181950,
    "dDurationMs": 2149,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You could watch it now if you haven’t."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184099,
    "dDurationMs": 3411,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I would wait, but you know where the pause button work."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187510,
    "dDurationMs": 6369,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’re going to start out by me totally convincing you that time must run slow in a gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193879,
    "dDurationMs": 3231,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field - an effect we call gravitational time\ndilation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197110,
    "dDurationMs": 7409,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But to do that I need to give you a quick\nrefresher on regular old time dilation, which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204519,
    "dDurationMs": 4071,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tells us moving clocks must appear to tick\nslowly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208590,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is from special relativity, rather than general relativity, but even special relativity\nseems a near miraculous insight."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215830,
    "dDurationMs": 4310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einstein also had help and built on prior\nand contemporary wisdom to develop it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220140,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it’s fair to say that relativity was\ndiscovered in his own imagination - in his"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226060,
    "dDurationMs": 2819,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "brilliant thought- or gedankenexperiments."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228879,
    "dDurationMs": 5481,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einstein’s thought laboratory - his gedankenlab - was filled with many incredible imaginary"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234360,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "devices, but one of his favorites was the\nphoton clock."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237440,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a simple pair of perfectly reflective,\nmassless mirrors between which bounces a single"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242540,
    "dDurationMs": 1319,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "photon of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243859,
    "dDurationMs": 3671,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A counter ticks over every time the photon\ndoes a full cycle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247530,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The photon clock represents the simplest possible clock, and anything that we conclude for it also"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252650,
    "dDurationMs": 1770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "applies to any other clock."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254420,
    "dDurationMs": 5659,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And, in fact, to any matter - anything that can experience time, which in practice means anything"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260079,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261079,
    "dDurationMs": 2391,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve talked about why this is the case\npreviously."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263470,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The amount of time taken for one tick of the photon clock is the distance the photon travels"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267800,
    "dDurationMs": 5290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "divided by its speed - so twice separation\nof the mirrors divided by the speed of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273090,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But let’s say the gerdankenlab is moving\nat a constant velocity past a stationary physicist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280110,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They see the photon clock ticking, but the\nphoton travels a longer path."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 285470,
    "dDurationMs": 2770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How long does it take to execute that one\ntick?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288240,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here we have to invoke the great founding\naxiom of special relativity - that the speed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294020,
    "dDurationMs": 4679,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of light is always measured to be the same\nfor all observers, no matter their personal"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298699,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "speed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299699,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From the stationary perspective, the photon\nseems to travel further but it has to keep"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303889,
    "dDurationMs": 5481,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same speed - so it appears to take longer to complete a single up-down tick."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309370,
    "dDurationMs": 3330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Add an identical but stationary photon clock."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312700,
    "dDurationMs": 4779,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It seems to tick more than once for a single\ntick of the moving clock."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317479,
    "dDurationMs": 5761,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this apparent slowing of time appears for everything in the moving lab."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323240,
    "dDurationMs": 1870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the whole situation is symmetric."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325110,
    "dDurationMs": 5770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For an observer in the moving lab, it appears that the stationary clock is ticking slow."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330880,
    "dDurationMs": 3690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s because there’s no preferred notion\nof “stillness” in relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334570,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They see the world as moving, and themselves stationary."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 338650,
    "dDurationMs": 4889,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Time dilation due to motion is inevitable\nif we accept the axiom of the constancy of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343539,
    "dDurationMs": 1601,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the speed of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345140,
    "dDurationMs": 5149,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To get to gravitational time dilation all\nwe need to do is add in the equivalence principle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350289,
    "dDurationMs": 2041,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as our second axiom."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352330,
    "dDurationMs": 4899,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It tells us that whatever we conclude about\nthe passage of time in an accelerating frame"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357229,
    "dDurationMs": 3191,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "must also be true in a gravitational field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360420,
    "dDurationMs": 4230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To get an accelerating frame we could strap\nrockets to our gerdankenlab - and don’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364650,
    "dDurationMs": 1169,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "worry, we will."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365819,
    "dDurationMs": 5681,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But first, let’s try this - build our lab\ninto a giant, ring-shaped space station."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371500,
    "dDurationMs": 5470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we set it rotating at the right speed then\ncentripetal acceleration leads to some nice"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376970,
    "dDurationMs": 1390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "artificial gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378360,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s also suit up a physicist and have\nthem float in space at one spot as the space"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383740,
    "dDurationMs": 1179,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "station turns."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384919,
    "dDurationMs": 4291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They’re in a non-accelerating, or inertial\nframe of reference."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389210,
    "dDurationMs": 5900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We have a photon clock in the lab and an identical one with the physicist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395110,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One tick of either clock is very short, which\nmeans that over that interval the lab moves"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400270,
    "dDurationMs": 2850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only a tiny arc of the full circle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403120,
    "dDurationMs": 3269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we can approximate its motion as a straight\nline."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406389,
    "dDurationMs": 3921,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Over that brief interval we know perfectly\nwell what the time difference is between the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410310,
    "dDurationMs": 1740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "two frames of reference."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412050,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Both observers see the other’s time has\nslowed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 415210,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But after a full revolution, both observers\nask each other how many ticks their clock"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 420470,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ticked."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 421470,
    "dDurationMs": 5771,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it turns out that the stationary clock\ndid tick more - time slowed for the rotating case."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 427241,
    "dDurationMs": 6298,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This seems paradoxical, but the solution is\nthe same as it is for the twin paradox from our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 433539,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "previous episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434539,
    "dDurationMs": 5271,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The summary is this: two observers moving\nin straight lines to each other do perceive"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439810,
    "dDurationMs": 2690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the other as time-dilated - slowed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442500,
    "dDurationMs": 4610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But as soon as one of those observers changes direction, the symmetry is broken."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 447110,
    "dDurationMs": 5029,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the twin paradox, the twin traveling to\na nearby star and back has aged less even"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 452139,
    "dDurationMs": 3981,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "though both could see the other’s clock\nticking slowly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 456120,
    "dDurationMs": 5079,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can see that when we use a spacetime diagram to show how the traveler tracks the passage"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461199,
    "dDurationMs": 1341,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of time back on Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462540,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Her perception of what is “simultaneous”\nto current moment flips at the turnaround"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468340,
    "dDurationMs": 4180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "point, so that she misses a bunch of the ticks of her brothers clock."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472520,
    "dDurationMs": 3489,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here’s the spacetime diagram for our rotating\nlab."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476009,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now 2 dimensions of space instead of one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478889,
    "dDurationMs": 6071,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The spacetime path or worldline of the lab\nis a helix, and the lab’s perception of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484960,
    "dDurationMs": 2769,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“now” is this shifting plane."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 487729,
    "dDurationMs": 5311,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s easier to see if we just take a slice\nout of this - one dimension of space again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493040,
    "dDurationMs": 2460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now the worldline is.a sine wave."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495500,
    "dDurationMs": 5099,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The lines of constant time for the moving\nclock tilt back and forth, and as that line"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500599,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tilts it fast-fowards over the clock ticks\nof the stationary clock."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 505289,
    "dDurationMs": 2620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The source of acceleration doesn’t matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507909,
    "dDurationMs": 3820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You get the same result if you do strap rockets to the gendankenlab."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511729,
    "dDurationMs": 4651,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The photon in the accelerating clock has to\nchase the upper mirror some, increasing the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516380,
    "dDurationMs": 2539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distance it needs to travel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 518919,
    "dDurationMs": 4601,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the way down the lower mirror catches up to it, reducing the down-tick distance."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523520,
    "dDurationMs": 4950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But overall, the distance for a single up-down tick is larger in a linearly accelerating"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528470,
    "dDurationMs": 2412,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "frame compared to the inertial frame."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530882,
    "dDurationMs": 3644,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so what does all this have to do with\ngravity?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534526,
    "dDurationMs": 4534,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The equivalence principle demands that there’s no experiment that can distinguish between"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539060,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "acceleration and gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540860,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ergo someone standing in a gravitational field must experience the same sense of weight AND"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 546360,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same time dilation that you would get\nfrom being spun in a circle at the right radius"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551290,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and speed, or accelerated with linear acceleration\nequal to the gravitational acceleration."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556600,
    "dDurationMs": 3850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If both of our axioms are true - the constancy of the speed of light and the equivalence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560450,
    "dDurationMs": 4730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of acceleration and gravity, then time must\nrun slow in gravitational fields."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565180,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It kind of blows me away that you can calculate the difference of the flow of time between"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570800,
    "dDurationMs": 5330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an inertial and accelerating frame using pure special relativity with its kinematic time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576130,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dilation plus shifting reference frames, OR\nyou can use general relativity to calculate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582290,
    "dDurationMs": 5049,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the gravitational time dilation for the equivalent gravitational acceleration."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 587339,
    "dDurationMs": 2111,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And you get the same answers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589450,
    "dDurationMs": 3949,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You do have to be careful to choose the right relative distances between observers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593399,
    "dDurationMs": 4831,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the case of the twin paradox, gravitational time dilation gives the right relative time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598230,
    "dDurationMs": 6109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flows if you consider the traveling twin to\nbe in a gravitational well with a constant acceleration"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 604339,
    "dDurationMs": 2071,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equal to her spaceship’s acceleration."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 606410,
    "dDurationMs": 2110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But how deep in the well?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608520,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, as deep as the distance back to Earth - which is why the time dilation in this case is so huge, even"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 614700,
    "dDurationMs": 2319,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if the acceleration is mild."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617019,
    "dDurationMs": 4721,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another note of caution: be aware that circular orbital motion in a gravitational field is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621740,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very different from our rotating space station- then both gravitational time dilation and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626920,
    "dDurationMs": 2680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "kinematic time dilation play separate roles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629600,
    "dDurationMs": 6140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So is it some sort of cosmic coincidence that you get the same number with shifting reference"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635740,
    "dDurationMs": 2620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "frames as with artificial gravity?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 638360,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well no, it’s telling us that the source of the time dilation is fundamentally the same."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 643820,
    "dDurationMs": 1900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, this is all fine and good."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645720,
    "dDurationMs": 5230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve reasoned our way to seeing that gravitational\ntime dilation must be a thing if our axioms"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 650950,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are right."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651950,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that doesn’t feel entirely satisfying\n- it doesn’t seem explanatory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658070,
    "dDurationMs": 5860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What really is it about the gravitational\nfield that’s causing time to tick slow?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663930,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps the photon - or whatever light-speed quantum components make up matter - actually do"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669390,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have to travel further - between mirrors or between the forces binding matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674550,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So that photon clocks and matter do evolve more slowly in gravitational fields."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679510,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or is it that if you’re inside a gravitational\nfield, your sense of “now” is continually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684200,
    "dDurationMs": 4995,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sweeping forward compared to regions further outside the gravitational field?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689195,
    "dDurationMs": 6324,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sure, both of there are valid and there are even more ways to think about this - and no one of them is closer"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695519,
    "dDurationMs": 5331,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to reality - they are, in a sense, just our\nway to map the math to our intuition."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700850,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But ultimately, asking “why does gravity\nslow time” is a bit backwards."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706400,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A better question may be “why does slowed\ntime cause gravity”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 711040,
    "dDurationMs": 4950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The curvature of space by matter isn’t nearly enough to give gravity at the strength we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715990,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "feel it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 716990,
    "dDurationMs": 3740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You’re held in your chair right now by the curvature in time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720730,
    "dDurationMs": 3500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In short, you’re held down because your\nbutt is ticking faster than your head."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724230,
    "dDurationMs": 6099,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And I’ll show you exactly why that’s true\nreal soon, when we explore the tangled connections"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730329,
    "dDurationMs": 4022,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between time and gravity in a curved spacetime."
    } ]
  } ]
}
